## Abstract

This project investigates relationships between borrower characteristics and credit default using a reproducible synthetic sample. The analysis begins with descriptive statistics and comparison of defaulted and non-defaulted borrowers. It then estimates and validates four classification models: a single-factor logistic regression, a multivariable logistic regression, a probit regression and a shallow decision tree.

The models are evaluated on an independent test sample using ROC AUC, Brier score, log loss, confusion matrices, calibration by risk groups and the difference between training and test performance. Logistic-regression coefficients are interpreted through odds ratios, while variance inflation factors are used as a multicollinearity diagnostic.

The data are fully synthetic. The project demonstrates statistical modelling and validation methods and does not represent a production model or the portfolio of any institution.
